    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\nImprove how a web app works across different screen sizes.\r\n\r\n1. Resize the browser window and switch between **Desktop** and **Mobile** views. Identify specific issues using the **Responsiveness Checklist** in your [Unit Guide](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Znt4C-uU4F-fVcYYlwx6qD3XcGqky9nbDbV84Kzqu5g/edit?usp=sharing).\r\n\r\n2. Choose **one** issue to fix.\r\n\r\n3. In `prompt.md`, plan your goal, constraints, and any edge cases to avoid.\r\n\r\n4. Add `prompt.md` to the AI Tutor chat and have AI help you fix the issue.",
    "teacher_markdown": "Encourage students to act like test engineers by using the Responsiveness Checklist in their [Unit Guide](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Znt4C-uU4F-fVcYYlwx6qD3XcGqky9nbDbV84Kzqu5g/edit?usp=sharing).\r\n\r\n**The Screen Toggle Shortcut** ![](https://images.code.org/b4e120e30eb45037adae93b45c0e72bd-image-1774517608974.png)\r\n\r\nRemind students they can use the desktop/mobile toggle in the preview bar (next to the Run button) to quickly switch views. This helps them spot issues without manually resizing every time.\r\n\r\n**The Resize Detective:**  \r\nHave students resize their browser and ask: “What changes? What breaks?” Students should name issues using the checklist before prompting AI.\r\n\r\n**The One-Issue Rule:**  \r\nIf students try to fix everything, redirect them to one specific issue (e.g., navigation, text, images).\r\n\r\n**The Small Fix Mindset:**  \r\nIf students are stuck, prompt them to focus on small, clear fixes (e.g., stacking layout, resizing images, wrapping text), not a full redesign.",
    "display_name": "GameTech ",
    "bubble_choice_description": "A gaming tech website works well on desktop, but key elements break on smaller screens. Identify and fix responsiveness issues so it functions smoothly across devices.",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://images.code.org/de6b0648a1ef5d807047bf70abada8f8-hero.jpg",
